\section{Theory} 

\subsection{8-staged symmetric block cipher pipeline}

An eight staged symmetric block cipher crypto pipeline to improve advanced encryption standard based on meta DES, 3DES with P-Box S-Box.


The following image shows you an example of a symmetric cipher 8 staged encryption pipe and the corresponding decryption inverse pipe.

Before entering the encryption pipe, the file can be zipped to avoid huge amount of symmetric cipher blocks and after exiting the encryption pipe the file can be ascii encoded with base64 mime, uuencode, xxencode or hex16, because symmetric chiphered binary files might lose their block padding.

Implementation is based on my blog article: \href{https://area23-at.blogspot.com/2024/04/making-symetric-chiffer-encryption.html}{Making symmetric cipher encryption meta permutating again}\cite{c1}, including the follwing symmetric cipher algorithms:

\begin{itemize}
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Encryption_Standard}{Aes}, AesLight, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Encryption_Standard}{Rijndael}
\item \href{https://www.schneier.com/}{Bruce Schneier's} BlowFish, 2-Fish, 3-Fish
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camellia_(cipher)}{Camellia}, CamelliaLight
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAST-128}{Cast5}, Cast6
\item \href{https://nsa.gov/}{National security agency's} Des, 3-Des, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skipjack_(cipher)}{SkipJack}
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kupyna}{Dstu7624}
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GOST_(block_cipher)}{Ghost}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Data_Encryption_Algorithm}{Idea}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NOEKEON}{Noekeon}
\item \href{https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/RC2_(Blockchiffre)}{RC2}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RC5}{RC5}32, RC564, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RC6}{RC6}
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SEED}{SEED}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SM4_(cipher)}{SM4}
\item \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serpent_(cipher)}{Serpent}, Tnepres
\item \href{https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Encryption_Algorithm}{Tea}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XTEA}{XTea}
\item and my own simplest symmetric block cipher alogrithms: ZenMatrix, ZenMatrix2
\end{itemize}


\subsection{What are advantages and disadvantages of Symmetric Block Cipher}

\subsubsection{Advantages}
Since symmetric block cipher ciphers each block in the same encrypting way

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{SymmetricBlockCipher_Structure0.png}
\caption{\label{fig:SymmetricBlockCipherStructure}Symmetric BlockCipher Structure}
\end{figure}

parallel processing can be implemented quiet easy with average performance bust on huge multiprocessore machines.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{SymmetricBlockCipher_Structure1.png}
\caption{\label{fig:SymmetricBlockCipherParallelism}Symmetric BlockCipher Parallelism}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Disadvantages}

Since we know often concrete structure of a file header, 
because of more static tableized structure inside file header and the sepecific binary format signature (MIT magic cookie)

REPLAY attacs could be used by trying encode only the 1st symmetric cipher block with some heuristic headers and
possible keys.

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{SymmetricBlockCipher_Header_1st_Block.png}
\caption{\label{fig:SymmetricBlockCipherHeader1stBlock}Symmetric BlockCipher Header 1st Block}
\end{figure}


\subsubsection{Most blockcipher algorithms break on a lot of 0 byte inside}
Most blockcipher algorithms break, when you fill a > 2x BLOCKSIZE (byte)0 inside a text or some other file. You can generate such a NULL block with linux dd:
\begin{small}
\begin{verbatim}
sudo nice -n -17 dd if=/dev/zero of=/mnt/h/zeros.txt bs=4k  count=64
\end{verbatim}
\end{small}

That's why we have added a gzip, bzip2, zip before to compress illegal character blocks inside the file to encrypt.


\subsection{Mathematical theory}

Consider that there are full bijective desterministic inversible functions, 
where inverse function 

to  y_x = F(x,...) 

is 
x_y = f(y,..)

then inverse function 

to 
\begin{math}
y_x = {F( G( H( I( J( K( L( M( N(x, ...)))))))))
\end{math}

is 
\begin{math}
x_y = n( m( l( k( j( i( h( g( f(y,...))))))))).
\end{math}

You can see a mappings from ascii-8 $\Longrightarrow ascii-8,

also always as Matrix from R256 $\Longrightarrow R256 

or hexadecimal from Rx100 $\Longrightarrow Rx100

you can see mappings from UTF-8  $\Longrightarrow \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTF-8#:~:text=UTF%2D8%20is%20a%20character,than%20any%20alternative%20text%20encoding.}{UTF-8}

\dots

\subsection{ZenMatrix a simplest possible algortihm to basically understand symmetric blockcipher}

Starting from a no permutating 1-matrix, where projection is same as base,
ZenMatrix generates a permutating mapping with blocksize 16
a matrix with only one 1 per row and column (rest is 0)
to change position inside block and value offset.

\url{https://area23.at/net/Crypt/ZenMatrixVisualize.aspx}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{2026-01-25_ZenMatrixVisualize.png}
\caption{\label{fig:ZenMatrixVisualize}ZenMatrix Visualize}
\end{figure}


